---
name: ptt-pr-reply
description: "Release the answer to a review of this branch's local request: carry out the plan drafted with /ptt-pr-draft-reply in this conversation (commits, tests) and record the reply. Without such a draft, asks first. Argument: none or the request number N."
disable-model-invocation: true
---

# Reply to a review

The command, or an AtE naming these records, approves the records this skill
writes; other work, such as changing the branch, needs AtE (rules file) or a
draft the user saw (below). You write only your own new message files:
never edit a message or move a request.

The branch is the one the user names; otherwise the branch of the worktree
this holder's task works in (`worktrees/<topic>`; if several, list them and
ask). Run every git command from the workspace root with `git -C
<worktree>`; never ask the user to change directory. Request numbers may be
short (`4`, `pr-4`, `000004`). Without N, the request is this branch's one
open request waiting for its author; if there is none or several, list them
(`<records>/bin/pr list all --branch BRANCH`) and ask. With N, the request
must be in `pr/open`: if `<records>/bin/pr list all` shows it merged or
closed, report its state and decision and stop. A decided request takes no
draft and no reply, and a pending draft of it lapses; a follow-up is a new
request (`/ptt-pr-submit`).

After a draft of this request in this conversation (`/ptt-pr-draft-reply`),
this command, or an AtE naming it, approves exactly the drafted plan with
the user's changes: its commits, their tests, and recording the reply.
Carrying it out fills every `{fill: ...}`; nothing is recorded while one
remains. If it needs more than the plan, stop and ask.

Without a draft of it in this conversation (also after `/clear`), do
nothing yet; ask:

```
No draft for request NNNNNN in this conversation. Decide:
1. Draft first (recommended): /ptt-pr-draft-reply NNNNNN; nothing written
2. Do the whole chain: change the code per the comments, commit, record the reply
3. No decision, discuss further
```

## Carrying it out

Reply whenever the branch changes or a review needs an answer, without
waiting for a decision (decisions only gate merging and closing); a reply
pins the new head and returns the request to "waiting for review".

1. Code changes are commits on the same branch, checked as for submitting
   (`ptt-pr-submit`, steps 1-2): committed and clean (`git -C <worktree>
   status --porcelain` empty, `git diff --check <main> HEAD` clean), history
   atomic, and `git merge-base --is-ancestor <main> HEAD` succeeds; `<main>`
   is the request's repository's main branch in `<records>/pr/repos`.
   Before rewriting history, tag the old head `recovery/<reason>/<branch
   with / as ->` (`-2`, `-3` if taken), for `ptt-pr-cleanup` to remove
   later; after a rewrite give the new `base` if it changed, the old-to-new
   SHA map, and a `git range-diff <base>..<old head> <base>..<new head>`
   summary.
2. Write `MMMM-reply.md` (one more than the highest `MMMM`), answering every
   comment by ID (`C1: fixed in <sha>`, `C2: disagree, because ...`). List
   `MMMM-*` again; if another file has your number and is already
   recorded, rename yours to the next free one.
3. Record only it: `<records>/bin/ptt record -m "pr NNNNNN: reply MMMM" <file>`.

## Template

`MMMM-reply.md`:

```markdown
---
request: NNNNNN
message: MMMM
kind: reply
author: requester
session: <holder name>
date: <YYYY-MM-DD>
base: <full SHA; only if the base changed>
head: <full SHA of the branch tip now under review>
---

# Reply

C1: <answer>

## New commits
`git log --oneline <previous head>..<head>`, or "none".

## Verification
What was re-run for the new head, with results.
```
